{"parentUuid":"42be06fc-9d74-4567-97a4-134e10e69ffe","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7bbfa7a0-a194-4f1f-93d9-ca26bfc18685","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017j9ghp15fdojQ9NjD4pSdm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# ADR-004: Local Offload Orchestrator — Route Execution to Benny, Reserve the Planner for Strategy and Adjudication\n2\t\n3\t| Field      | Value                                                                                          |\n4\t| ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n5\t| Status     | Accepted (Phase 0–4 implemented; generate+judge verified live against Lemonade 2026-06-29)      |\n6\t| Date       | 2026-06-29                                                                                      |\n7\t| Authors    | Binary 16 (engineering authority)                                                              |\n8\t| Related    | ADR-001 (determinism boundary / agent_sandbox), opencode audit pattern, memo-ray token-audit   |\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 1. Problem\n13\t\n14\tDriving Prime-Silo development through a cloud planning agent (Claude Code,\n15\tAntigravity) burns credits/tokens on work a local model could do. The operator's\n16\thypothesis: **≥75% of execution tasks are offloadable**, which would free the\n17\tplanner's budget for planning and strategy.\n18\t\n19\t## 2. The one insight that shapes the design\n20\t\n21\t**Offloading *execution* to a local model does not, by itself, save the planner's\n22\ttokens.** The expensive resource is tokens flowing **through the planner's\n23\tcontext** — so if the planner reads the full local output back, nothing is saved.\n24\tThe savings come from three disciplines:\n25\t\n26\t1. The planner writes a **compact manifest** instead of doing the work.\n27\t2. An **evaluation gate** absorbs verbose output **locally**.\n28\t3. The planner reads back only a **digest + verdict**, and is escalated to only on\n29\t   failure or ambiguity.\n30\t\n31\tThis reframes \"MCP sub-agent vs. async queue\": it is **both, behind one gate**.\n32\tThe async/manifest lane is what unlocks the bulk; the gate is what makes it safe.\n33\t\n34\t## 3. Decision\n35\t\n36\tA **local-first executor with an audit gate**, composed from primitives Prime-Silo\n37\talready has (workflow manifests, the integration-audit pattern, the vision\n38\tfidelity judge, the MCP server, the `resolve_executor` local-model layer):\n39\t\n40\t- **Contract** — `aamp.offload_task/1` (`manifests/offload/task.manifest.schema.json`).\n41\t  Planner authors `intent` + testable `acceptance_criteria` + `risk_tier`.\n42\t- **Router** (`benny/core/offload/router.py`, `router.matrix.json`) — classifies\n43\t  green / yellow / red. May **upgrade** a tier, never silently downgrades.\n44\t  Guarded paths (`L1/ L2/ manifests/`, signing) and security intents force red.\n45\t- **Executor** (`executor.py`) — `shell` (codemods/scaffolds) or `generate` (local\n46\t  model via `resolve_executor`). Output is a *proposed artifact*, never a direct\n47\t  write to the deterministic zone.\n48\t- **Gate** (`gate.py`) — deterministic checks first (build/lint/type/test, every\n49\t  `verify` command, all must exit 0); then, for yellow only, an **LLM judge**\n50\t  scores the artifact against the acceptance criteria.\n51\t- **Orchestrator** (`orchestrator.py`) — ties submit → route → execute → gate →\n52\t  digest; persists the full artifact to the workspace **outbox** and returns only\n53\t  a compact **digest** to the planner.\n54\t- **Ledger** (`ledger.py`) — append-only JSONL of honest components for\n55\t  measurement.\n56\t\n57\t### Two lanes, one gate\n58\t\n59\t- **Sync (MCP):** `offload_exec` tool → `POST /api/offload/submit?wait=1` → digest\n60\t  inline. For small bounded tasks the planner needs now.\n61\t- **Async (queue):** `enqueue` → inbox → `scripts/offload-runner.mjs` drains →\n62\t  outbox + digest. The default for bulk work — the \"less synchronous\" path.\n63\t\n64\t## 4. The routing rule\n65\t\n66\t> **If you can write crisp, testable acceptance criteria up front, the task is\n67\t> offloadable. If defining \"done\" needs judgment, the planner keeps it.**\n68\t\n69\t| Tier      | Examples                                                            | Handling                         |\n70\t| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------- |\n71\t| 🟢 green  | scaffolds, codemods, test stubs, doc-gen, formatting, dep bumps     | deterministic gate only          |\n72\t| 🟡 yellow | feature against a spec, bug fix with a repro, multi-file edit       | deterministic gate **+ judge**   |\n73\t| 🔴 red    | architecture, ambiguous reqs, security/signing, deterministic zone  | **escalate — never offloaded**   |\n74\t\n75\t**`shell` vs `generate` — what the gate can actually validate.** A `shell` task\n76\tacts *in place*, so the deterministic gate validates the real effect → it can be\n77\ttrue green. A `generate` task produces an *unapplied proposal* in the outbox\n78\t(ADR-001); deterministic checks run against the unchanged repo and cannot validate\n79\tit. So the router **upgrades every generate task to yellow** and the gate\n80\t**refuses to auto-pass** a generate proposal without a judge — the judge reading\n81\tthe artifact is the only valid evaluator for unapplied output. (Found while\n82\tpreparing to run a `generate` docstring task on real code: it would have \"passed\"\n83\tdeterministic checks against the stale file — a false pass this rule prevents.)\n84\t\n85\t## 5. Anti-collusion\n86\t\n87\tThe judge model SHOULD differ from the executor model; same-model self-judging\n88\trubber-stamps (the strawman failure mode from the memo-ray token-audit). The gate\n89\t**flags** `judge == executor` and treats that judgment as low-confidence. The\n90\tdeterministic checks are the hard backstop the judge can never override.\n91\t\n92\t**Judge reliability is the current weak link (measured).** See\n93\t`manifests/offload/JUDGE-CALIBRATION.md`. On this box the small local judges are\n94\tnot reliable enough to *pass* work: the 0.5B false-positived broken code (0.9), so\n95\tthe default judge is `Phi-4-mini-instruct-NPU` whose failure mode is *safe* (it\n96\trejects bad work / escalates good work rather than rubber-stamping). Consequence:\n97\t**reliable savings come from GREEN deterministic tasks**; YELLOW judged tasks often\n98\tescalate even when good, until a more capable non-reasoning judge model is\n99\tavailable. `run_judge` forces `response_format: json_object`, retries once, and\n100\tparses the last balanced JSON to maximize what reliability is achievable.\n101\t\n102\t**Pick a fast *non-reasoning* instruct model as judge.** Live finding (2026-06-29):\n103\t`deepseek-r1-8b-FLM` as judge spent its whole token budget reasoning in prose and\n104\tnever emitted the JSON verdict — so every judgment came back unscored and escalated,\n105\tdefeating the gate. Reasoning models belong on the **executor** (hard generation),\n106\tnot the judge (quick structured scoring). `run_judge` is hardened to survive a\n107\treasoning judge anyway — it requests thinking-off (`enable_thinking: false`), gives a\n108\tlarger budget, and parses the **last** balanced JSON object (ignoring leading\n109\tchain-of-thought / `<think>` blocks) — but a small instruct judge (e.g.\n110\t`Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct-CPU`, the default) is faster and more reliable.\n111\t\n112\t## 6. ADR-001 boundary (why this is safe)\n113\t\n114\tThe executor writes only into `$BENNY_HOME/workspaces/<ws>/offload/` (scratch /\n115\toutbox). Promotion of a passing result into `manifests/` / L1 / L2 stays a\n116\t**human-signed** step — the existing `drafts/ → HITL → sign_manifest()` flow. The\n117\tgate is the machine pre-filter in front of the human gate, not a replacement.\n118\t\n119\t## 7. Honesty / measurement\n120\t\n121\tPer the memo-ray token-audit lesson, **measure** the savings, don't assert them.\n122\t`scripts/offload-report.mjs` reports the **offload rate** (passed locally without\n123\tescalation), the **read-back cost** (digest chars the planner consumed), and a\n124\tclearly-labelled **estimate** of saved completion tokens. 75% is a target to reach\n125\tin the ledger, not a number to claim.\n126\t\n127\t## 8. Status / what is and isn't verified\n128\t\n129\t- ✅ Phase 0 contract; router; deterministic gate; orchestrator control-flow;\n130\t  red-escalation; outbox/digest discipline; ledger; MCP tool; runner; report —\n131\t  all implemented and covered by `tests/core/test_offload.py` (network-free).\n132\t- ✅ **Verified live (2026-06-29):** end-to-end `generate` + judge against\n133\t  Lemonade. Default executor `qwen3.5-9b-FLM` produced clean, correct code\n134\t  (no reasoning-dump), judge `Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct-CPU` (distinct model,\n135\t  `collusion: False`) scored 0.95 → passed in 1 iteration, digest 512 chars while\n136\t  the artifact stayed in the outbox. (`qwen3-tk-4b-FLM` also passed at 0.85 but\n137\t  buried the code in chain-of-thought — hence 9b is the default.) An earlier run\n138\t  confirmed honest **escalation** when the executor model 500'd (GGUF/llamacpp +\n139\t  NPU recipes fail to load `llama-server` on this box).\n140\t- ⚠️ **Still unverified:** the actual offload *rate* on a real task stream — the\n141\t  ledger is the instrument to validate the 75% hypothesis; needs volume.\n142\t\n143\t## 9. Consequences\n144\t\n145\t- The planner's budget shifts toward planning/adjudication.\n146\t- New surface to maintain (router matrix, gate). Mitigated by reuse + tests.\n147\t- Risk: output bloat back into context — mitigated by strict digest discipline\n148\t  (enforced + tested). Risk: silent quality erosion — tracked via escalation rate\n149\t  and (future) defect-escape rate in the ledger.\n150\t"}]},"uuid":"9947f71f-184a-428b-b10f-34a10df50fc4","t